\description{
The design twin of \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}: the same table of means,
standard deviations, intervals and counts, computed from a
\code{survey::svydesign()} or \code{survey::as.svrepdesign()} object instead
of a data frame.

Not one statistic is computed here. \code{survey::svymean()} gives the
mean, its standard error and its design effect, \code{survey::svyvar()}
the standard deviation, \code{survey::svyquantile()} the quantiles, and
\code{survey::svyttest()} / \code{survey::regTermTest()} /
\code{survey::svyranktest()} the group comparison. Every interval and
every test is referred to \code{survey::degf(design)}.
}
\section{Which function do I need?}{
A data frame with a column of weights is \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}
(\verb{weights = }). A survey design object -- strata, clusters, finite
population correction, calibration, replicate weights -- is this
function. Passing one to the other errors with the name of the
right one; there is no silent coercion, because the design-based
standard errors, degrees of freedom and tests cannot be recovered
from the weights alone.
}

\section{Two conventions, one bridge}{
\code{table_continuous(weights = )} implements the \strong{frequency-expansion}
convention: a weight is a number of copies, and \code{SD} has denominator
\code{sum(w) - 1}. This function implements the \strong{sampling-weight}
convention: a weight is a number of units represented, and \code{SD} is
\code{sqrt(survey::svyvar())}, whose denominator is \code{n - 1} on weights
normalized to sum to \code{n}. These are two estimands, not two
approximations of one.

\code{rescale = TRUE} is the bridge, and it is an identity rather than a
coincidence. Writing \verb{w' = w * n / sum(w)}, the rescaled weighted
variance is
\verb{sum(w' (x - xbar)^2) / (sum(w') - 1) = n / (n - 1) * sum(w (x - xbar)^2) / sum(w)},
which is what \code{survey::svyvar()} computes. So on a design that
declares nothing but weights, \code{table_continuous(weights = w, rescale = TRUE)} and this function return the same mean and the same
standard deviation. The default \code{rescale = FALSE} does not, and that
is the estimand boundary, not a bug.

The mean is continuous across both regimes: \verb{sum(w x) / sum(w)} does
not move when the weights are rescaled.
}

\section{Choosing the statistics}{
\code{show_columns} takes the tokens of \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}} with two
additions and one removal:
\itemize{
\item \code{"se"} -- the design-based standard error of the mean;
\item \code{"deff"} -- the design effect (requires \code{deff = TRUE});
\item \code{"med_ci"} is refused. The exact interval of the sibling inverts a
binomial sign test on independent observations, which a clustered
or stratified sample is not.
}
}

\section{Quantiles}{
\code{qrule = "math"} is the default and estimates \verb{inf\{x : F(x) >= p\}},
the quantile of the \emph{population}. \code{qrule = "spicy"} switches to the
type-7 interpolation \code{table_continuous()} uses, for a reader who
needs the two tables to agree cell for cell; any other value --
including a function -- is handed to \code{survey::svyquantile()}
untouched. The note always says which rule produced the numbers.
}

\section{Groups and degrees of freedom}{
\verb{by = } cuts one domain per group with \code{[} on the design. survey
recomputes the degrees of freedom on the primary sampling units and
strata each domain retains, so a grouped table generally carries a
\emph{different} df per row; the note gives the span when they differ.

A group with a missing value is a domain like any other:
\code{drop_na = FALSE} gives it a \code{(Missing)} row, with its own degrees
of freedom. A domain reduced to one primary sampling unit has none,
and its interval shows the undefined dash rather than an interval
built on \code{qt(p, df = 0)}.

The comparison is a single test on the whole design, not a set of
pairwise ones: \code{survey::svyttest()} with two observed groups,
\code{survey::regTermTest()} on \code{survey::svyglm()} with three or more,
or \code{survey::svyranktest()} under \code{test = "nonparametric"}. Under a
design the Welch / Student distinction does not exist -- the
variance is the design's -- so \code{test = "student"} warns and behaves
like \code{"welch"}. A variable with no variance, or whose domains are each
constant on their own value (a residual variance of zero), has no
comparison, with a warning (class \code{spicy_undefined_stat}), where survey
would still return a p-value. Under \code{test = "nonparametric"} too, unlike
\code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}: \code{survey::svyranktest()} is a design-based test of
the ranks, which are then constant within the domains as well. Its
test cells show the undefined mark,
as do those of any comparison that cannot run (too few observations in
a domain, a test refused under weights that change sign).
The note names the comparison of each variable.

A variable with no observed value in a domain (a question not asked
in that group) has no data in it, as in \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}: its
cells there show an en dash, \emph{n} included, those rows are not counted
in the missing values of the note, the comparison uses the domains
with data, and the note says so ("No data for X in G.").
}

\section{Stability}{
This function is \strong{stabilizing} in the sense \code{?spicy} defines: the
names of its design-specific arguments may still be tightened
before 1.0 -- with a \code{NEWS.md} entry -- but the behavior does not
change silently. It was experimental through 0.13; the shape of the
table has held across the cycle, so it now moves on the parent
family's clock rather than its own. The numbers themselves are
survey's and do not move with it.
}

\section{What is absent, and why}{
\code{weights} and \code{rescale} (the weighting \emph{is} the design), \code{effect_size}
and \code{smd} (no established design-based variance), and \code{data}.
}

\section{Multiple-comparison adjustment}{
The p-values of a descriptive table describe: they test no
prespecified hypothesis. STROBE (explanation of item 14) and the
SAMPL guidelines advise against significance tests in descriptive and
baseline tables, hence the default \code{p_adjust = "none"}.

When a journal or a protocol asks for adjusted p-values anyway,
\code{p_adjust} applies \code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}} to the family of every test in
the table: one p-value per variable (one per block in
\code{\link[=table_outcome]{table_outcome()}}), never the rows of one variable, which share one
test. \code{"holm"} suits a confirmatory set of comparisons, \code{"BH"} a
screening one; \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}} lists the methods. A test that
returned no p-value stays out of the family.

The adjustment runs before any formatting, so the \code{p} column and its
\verb{<.001} threshold show the adjusted values, and the table note gives
the number of tests and the method. The raw p-values stay in the
object: a \code{p_unadjusted} column next to the p-value column of the
returned data frames, and \code{col_meta$p$p_unadjusted} (with the method
in \code{p_adjust} and the family size in \code{p_adjust_m}) in
\code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}}. Without \code{by}, or with \code{p_value = FALSE}, the table
shows no p-value to adjust, and a \code{p_adjust} other than \code{"none"} is an
error.
}

